{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["SRC_008 discusses a continuous outcome space and high-dimensional features; here the outcome is objectively verifiable, which may simplify the structure and reduce the penalty of dimensionality reduction.","SRC_008 frames information loss as a model property; here the information loss is procedural—a deliberate design choice in the checklist.","The panel in this problem aggregates multiple non-experts, which can partially recover information lost by individual checklist compliance through stochastic variation in interpretation, whereas SRC_008 does not involve ensemble averaging."],"overturn_conditions":["If empirical evaluation shows the panel outperforming the expert on adversarial items designed to break the checklist, the dimensionality-reduction framing is insufficient.","If the expert's intuition is shown to be a low-dimensional heuristic masquerading as high-dimensional reasoning, the analogy to SRC_008's information-rich high-dimensional space fails.","If the aggregation of non-experts is shown to recover information not present in the checklist via emergent collective intelligence, the bottleneck model is incomplete."],"shared_structure":["Both involve a structural bottleneck: SRC_008's argument about dimensionality reduction and information loss mirrors the checklist's projection from the expert's high-dimensional feature space to a low-dimensional checklist space.","Both depend on task structure: the severity of information loss in SRC_008 depends on the outcome space's topology, just as the checklist's fidelity depends on the outcome-determination function's complexity.","In both, the apparent advantage of the reduced system (panel/checklist or the reduced model) is conditional on the sufficiency of the retained dimensions."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_008","SRC_001"],"transfer_claims":["SRC_008's argument that dimensionality reduction causes information loss transfers directly: the checklist is a dimensionality-reduction tool, and its reliability gain is bounded by the information retained.","The condition under which the panel wins is the same as the condition under which SRC_008's 'against' case weakens: when the outcome is determined by a structure that the reduced representation (checklist) captures fully.","The diagnostic for distinguishing genuine reliability from compliance tracking—testing on items where the reduced representation is misleading—transfers from SRC_008's logic of probing the high-dimensional space with cases the low-dimensional model gets wrong."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The checklist's apparent reliability advantage is a separability artifact: it transfers reliability gains only in task regions where the checklist items correspond one-to-one with the objectively-verifiable outcome structure. Single experts capture latent cues the checklist ignores, so panel reliability collapses exactly when an item's checklist-compliance surface and its outcome-determination surface diverge. An evaluation distinguishes a genuine reliability gain from mere compliance tracking by testing on adversarially-constructed items where checklist compliance is achievable by a misleading subset of features.","counterconditions":["If the expert's intuition is systematically biased (e.g., overweighting salient but non-diagnostic cues), the checklist's information loss is beneficial, and the panel will outperform the expert even outside the checklist subspace.","If the task outcome is truly determined by a small, linear, non-interacting set of features, the checklist projection is information-preserving and the panel will match or exceed the expert without any subspace collapse.","If the expert's 'intuition' is actually a well-rehearsed internal checklist, the dichotomy between checklist and intuition is false and the panel's only advantage is aggregation noise reduction."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A checklist defines a low-dimensional projection of the outcome-determining feature space. Trained non-experts using it will reliably produce correct answers only when that projection is information-preserving with respect to the outcome class. A domain expert relies on intuition, which is a high-dimensional model encoding latent feature combinations that the checklist eliminates. The panel's reliability gain is genuine only within the task subspace spanned by the checklist. Outside it, the panel exhibits high agreement (because all members apply the same checklist) but low accuracy, while the expert's variance increases but their mean accuracy degrades less because their implicit model retains more outcome-relevant dimensions. Thus the panel's 'reliability' is often a reliability of process, not of outcome.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","ef9e8bf30aa86a0e9faf2c1b7f60c98286c545a62190e809bd8bbbb51d0ac4f1"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["SRC_005 and SRC_006 frame the analogy source as a mechanical or implicit model context; here the mechanism is statistical aggregation theory.","The source artifacts treat the checklist as a fixed artifact; this candidate treats checklist compliance as a stochastic process with decomposable bias and variance.","The target problem asks for a diagnostic within a single evaluation, which requires a within-evaluation statistical decomposition not present in the source analogies."],"overturn_conditions":["If the expert's intuition is shown to be decomposable into a shared prior plus idiosyncratic noise, the distinction between expert and panel collapses and the checklist is irrelevant.","If panel errors are shown to be perfectly correlated, the aggregation mechanism fails and the panel is equivalent to a single non-expert.","If the checklist is shown to be a source of idiosyncratic noise rather than a shared prior, the bias-variance decomposition proposed here is backwards."],"shared_structure":["SRC_005 discusses a panel of non-experts using a checklist, which is the target situation; the mechanism of aggregation noise reduction is implicit in the ensemble.","SRC_006 notes the panel uses an explicit checklist while source contexts involve implicit models, which mirrors the distinction between the shared prior (checklist) and individual interpretation noise.","Both this candidate and SRC_005/SRC_006 frame the panel's advantage as a property of the group process rather than individual competence."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_006"],"transfer_claims":["The idea that a panel of non-experts can approximate expert performance transfers from SRC_005/SRC_006, with the refinement that the approximation quality depends on error correlation.","The distinction between explicit checklist and implicit models from SRC_006 transfers as the distinction between shared bias (from the checklist) and idiosyncratic noise (from interpretation).","The diagnostic—examining error covariance—transfers the idea from the sources that the panel's advantage is structural, not individual, and must be evaluated as a group property."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel's reliability gain is an aggregation effect, but it is genuine only if checklist compliance is stochastic rather than deterministic. If every panel member applies the checklist identically, the panel is a single non-expert with extra cost; the reliability gain emerges from the checklist serving as a shared prior that biases individual judgments toward the outcome while independent interpretation noise cancels. The diagnostic within a single evaluation is the covariance structure of panel errors: genuine reliability gain requires errors to be uncorrelated across members conditional on the checklist, while mere compliance produces perfectly correlated errors.","counterconditions":["If panel members' interpretations of checklist items are highly correlated (e.g., due to shared training or common misconceptions), aggregation provides no benefit and the panel reduces to a single biased classifier.","If the expert's intuition is highly reliable and low-variance, the panel's noise-cancellation advantage is outweighed by the expert's superior signal.","If the checklist is so prescriptive that it leaves no room for interpretation, there is no idiosyncratic noise to cancel, and the panel offers no advantage over a single non-expert using the checklist."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The checklist does not replace judgment; it constrains it. Each non-expert still maps checklist criteria to a final answer. If this mapping has a systematic bias, the panel's mean is biased. If the mapping has idiosyncratic noise, aggregation cancels it. The checklist increases the ratio of shared bias to idiosyncratic variance. Compared to a single expert, the panel wins when the expert's idiosyncratic noise (unreliable intuition) exceeds the checklist's systematic bias. The expert wins when the checklist's bias exceeds the expert's noise. 'Mere checklist compliance tracking the right answer' is the degenerate case where the checklist bias is zero and noise is zero—perfect reliability but no generalization. The genuine gain is when the checklist bias is small and noise cancels, yielding high accuracy the expert cannot achieve due to their own volatility.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["SRC_007 concludes that the perturbation design cannot satisfy its own success criterion, which may undermine the practicality of this candidate's diagnostic.","SRC_007 is framed as a critique of a specific perturbation design; this candidate generalizes perturbation as a general diagnostic principle.","The source context may involve trained individuals; here the subjects are non-experts, which changes the interpretation of perturbation results."],"overturn_conditions":["If the checklist is shown to be robust to all practically achievable perturbations, the causal vs. correlational distinction may not matter for reliability.","If the expert's intuition is shown to be equally vulnerable to perturbations, the diagnostic does not distinguish panel from expert reliability.","If perturbation testing is shown to be impossible for the class of tasks in question, this candidate's diagnostic is untestable."],"shared_structure":["SRC_007 discusses adversarial perturbations that modify inputs to satisfy checklist items, which is exactly the counterfactual test proposed here for distinguishing causal tracking from compliance.","SRC_001's mechanist stance supports the assumption that reliability must be grounded in causal mechanisms, not just correlations.","Both this candidate and SRC_007 use perturbation as a probe for the validity of checklist-based judgments."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_007","SRC_001"],"transfer_claims":["The perturbation-based diagnostic from SRC_007 transfers directly: if you can perturb an item to satisfy the checklist without changing the outcome, the panel is tracking compliance.","The mechanist assumption from SRC_001 transfers: reliability requires tracking the causal mechanism, not just satisfying a procedural checklist.","The failure mode identified in SRC_007—that perturbations cannot satisfy their own success criterion—transfers as a warning that the diagnostic may be practically limited."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The reliability gain is genuine only if the checklist encodes causal features of the outcome and mere compliance tracks correlates. The diagnostic is perturbation invariance: a genuine checklist's items, when satisfied, should not be bypassable by spurious changes that leave the causal determinants of the outcome untouched. If a perturbation can satisfy the checklist while changing the outcome, the panel is tracking compliance, not reliability.","counterconditions":["If the outcome is determined by a complex interaction of features such that no simple causal feature list exists, the checklist cannot encode causal determinants and the causal vs. correlational distinction is moot.","If the expert's intuition is itself tracking correlates rather than causes, the expert has no advantage over a bad checklist.","If the perturbation required to test causal vs. correlational structure is practically impossible or alters the task so much that the checklist is no longer applicable, the diagnostic is unimplementable."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Objectively-verifiable outcomes have causal determinants. A well-constructed checklist operationalizes these determinants. A poorly constructed one operationalizes correlates. Non-experts following a good checklist are reliable because they are checking the causal chain. Non-experts following a bad checklist are compliant but unreliable because they are checking a spurious chain. The expert's intuition, if reliable, implicitly tracks the causal structure. The diagnostic is therefore counterfactual: modify an item so that the checklist is still satisfied but the causal determinants of the outcome are altered. If the panel's judgment does not change, it is tracking checklist compliance. If it changes, the checklist is encoding genuine causal signal.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","a3589e2ab8672593dfc804e0b2a78fe4c47f889b542a4f435ee540a7588c8e57"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["SRC_004 may allow for inference from observable outcomes to underlying mechanisms; this candidate denies that such inference is possible without adversarial items.","SRC_002's skepticism may be directed at the panel's reliability; this candidate's skepticism is directed at the evaluation's ability to distinguish reliability types.","The sources may assume the evaluation can be designed to test the distinction; this candidate makes the design of the evaluation a necessary condition."],"overturn_conditions":["If a reliable measure of 'checklist understanding' or 'causal tracking' can be extracted from panel behavior on standard items, the underdetermination claim fails.","If the expert's behavior provides a baseline that allows distinguishing checklist compliance from genuine reasoning (e.g., by comparing error patterns), the single evaluation may be sufficient.","If the checklist's structure is transparent enough that its causal validity can be assessed a priori, empirical adversarial testing is unnecessary."],"shared_structure":["SRC_004's empiric stance supports the claim that only observable outcomes matter, and that unobservable constructs like 'genuine reliability' vs 'compliance' are not directly accessible.","SRC_002's skeptic stance supports the doubt that a single evaluation can establish a deep distinction like genuine vs. spurious reliability.","Both this candidate and the empiric/skeptic sources restrict what can be concluded from a single finite evaluation."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_004","SRC_002"],"transfer_claims":["The empiricist restriction to observable outcomes transfers as the claim that only correctness on adversarial items counts as evidence.","The skeptic's doubt transfers as the claim that without adversarial items, the evaluation cannot distinguish the two hypotheses.","The need for a specific evaluation design (adversarial items) transfers from the combined stance that evidence is distribution-dependent."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The distinction between genuine reliability gain and mere checklist compliance tracking the right answer is a false dichotomy. Within a single evaluation on tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, the only observable is correctness. 'Genuine reliability' and 'lucky compliance' are indistinguishable unless the evaluation includes out-of-distribution or adversarial items. The evidence that distinguishes them is therefore not a property of the evaluation itself but of the evaluation's item distribution: if the items cover cases where the checklist and the outcome diverge, compliance tracking fails and genuine reliability is revealed. If no such items exist, the question is unanswerable.","counterconditions":["If the checklist is derived from a valid causal model of the outcome, then compliance tracking is genuine reliability by definition, and the distinction collapses in favor of the panel.","If the expert's intuition is also merely tracking correlates, then neither the panel nor the expert has 'genuine' reliability, and the question is moot.","If the single evaluation is large and diverse enough to approximate the full task distribution, then in-distribution performance is a sufficient proxy for genuine reliability, and the distinction is practically meaningless."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Any finite set of objectively-verifiable outcomes can be matched by a sufficiently complex checklist that merely correlates with the answers. The distinction between genuine and spurious reliability requires a generalization test, which by definition cannot be fully contained in a single evaluation unless that evaluation is explicitly designed to include the distribution of cases where the checklist's heuristic fails. The 'single evaluation' constraint therefore makes the problem underdetermined unless the evaluation's item space is adversarially structured. The evidence is the presence of adversarial items in the evaluation set, not a property of the panel or the checklist alone.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["SRC_008 discusses continuous outcome space; here the outcome is objectively verifiable, which may make the lossy compression more or less damaging depending on task structure.","SRC_008 may not involve a panel; the aggregation effect is specific to this problem and not in the source.","The checklist is a deliberately designed lossy compression, whereas SRC_008's dimensionality reduction may be an unavoidable modeling choice."],"overturn_conditions":["If the checklist is shown to be a non-lossy compression of the expert's model, the information-loss argument fails.","If the panel's aggregation is shown to recover pruned features through emergent collective behavior, the loss is not irreversible.","If the expert's intuition is shown to be less accurate than the checklist on a broad range of tasks, the compression argument is backwards."],"shared_structure":["SRC_008's argument about information loss from dimensionality reduction directly supports the claim that the checklist loses information the expert retains.","SRC_001's mechanist stance supports modeling intuition as a compressed representation of the outcome structure.","Both this candidate and SRC_008 frame the comparison as one between a high-dimensional model (expert) and a low-dimensional reduction (checklist)."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_008","SRC_001"],"transfer_claims":["The information-loss argument from SRC_008 transfers as the checklist's pruning of expert-relevant features.","The mechanist view from SRC_001 transfers as the model of expert intuition as a high-dimensional compressed representation.","The prediction that the panel's reliability collapses on cases requiring pruned features transfers from SRC_008's logic of high-dimensional structure."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A trained non-expert panel using a checklist will, on average, be less reliable than a single domain expert. The expert's intuition is a compressed representation of the outcome-determining structure built from years of feedback. The checklist is a lossy compression of the expert's knowledge. The panel adds noise reduction but cannot recover the information lost by the checklist. Therefore, the panel's reliability is bounded above by the checklist's fidelity, which is lower than the expert's. The diagnostic for mere compliance is that panel accuracy will be high on items the checklist covers and collapse on items requiring the information the checklist discarded, which a single evaluation must include to detect.","counterconditions":["If the expert's intuition is itself a lossy compression that is worse than the checklist's compression (e.g., due to bias), the panel wins.","If the task is simple enough that the checklist is not lossy, the panel matches the expert and may win on noise reduction.","If the non-experts bring independent information not in the checklist and not in the expert's intuition, the panel could outperform both, but this contradicts the 'non-expert' assumption."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expert intuition is not magic; it is implicit model averaging over a high-dimensional feature space. A checklist is an attempt to make this model explicit, but in doing so, it prunes features deemed non-essential. These pruned features are essential for a non-trivial subset of cases. Non-experts using the checklist cannot access these features. The panel's aggregation reduces judgment noise but cannot recover lost signal. Thus, on cases where the pruned features matter, the panel is systematically wrong with high agreement, while the expert is correct because their implicit model retained those features. The panel's 'reliability' is thus a form of overconfidence in a lossy model.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","0ea28e52af1cb50a4a337346460ec969626ef75743d9ebcb030c94d9015a8734"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55}